{
 "rows": [
  {
   "definition": "Read-only access to the BeatScript ontology (https://beatscript.app/ontology/ns#), derived from the bitwrap ontology 0.2.0: genres, scales, grooves, song sections, instruments, Euclidean rhythms, Markov melody, the Petri-net engine concepts, and the BeatScript language's own terms (grammar productions, keywords, voices, kits, control nouns and verbs, MCP tools). Queries run over the materialized graph and are capped at 1000 rows and 5 seconds.",
   "label": "BeatScript ontology MCP server",
   "term": "https://beatscript.app/ontology/concept/product-beatscript-ontology-mcp"
  },
  {
   "definition": "A rhythm made by the Bjorklund algorithm. Many traditional patterns around the world are Euclidean rhythms. In the engine it becomes a rhythm ring with a note binding on each pulse step.",
   "label": "Euclidean rhythm",
   "term": "https://beatscript.app/ontology/concept/euclidean-rhythm"
  },
  {
   "definition": "A rhythm made by the Bjorklund algorithm. Many traditional patterns around the world are Euclidean rhythms. In the engine it becomes a rhythm ring with a note binding on each pulse step.",
   "definition_from": "https://beatscript.app/ontology/concept/euclidean-rhythm",
   "label": "Euclidean rhythm",
   "term": "https://beatscript.app/ontology/ns#EuclideanRhythm"
  },
  {
   "definition": "The arrange mutation operator euclid.",
   "label": "euclid",
   "term": "https://beatscript.app/ontology/concept/mutation-op-euclid"
  },
  {
   "definition": "The pattern generator euclid: a call that makes a pattern from its arguments.",
   "label": "euclid",
   "term": "https://beatscript.app/ontology/concept/generator-euclid"
  },
  {
   "definition": "K hits spread evenly over N steps, as a Euclidean rhythm.",
   "label": "euclid term",
   "term": "https://beatscript.app/ontology/ns#EuclidTerm"
  },
  {
   "definition": "Generate a whole BeatScript track from a seed, with no model and no sign-in: the beats generator picks the genre's rhythms (Euclidean), chord progression, instruments and song structure from the seed, and the result is printed as readable source (named lets, a def for the shared groove, sections, an arrange, filter ramps, the chords as Roman numerals and e()/arp()/markov()/every/rotate/degrade with their seeds written out, so one `key` line moves the whole song) for you to edit. Same genre, bpm, key, length and seed: the same text and CID, every time; the seed and generator are in the #! header so the track can be re-derived. The program passes check and is stored as public CC0 (source generate). Answers text, cid, name, seed, generator, url and render_url; play it with eval or load, or edit it and check again.\n\nThe answer also has gen, the call that is this program in BeatScript (gen@2 techno seed=42, with the bpm=, key= and length= you gave: `arrange from gen@2 techno seed=42` plays it), expanded_cid (the same as cid: a generated program has no references), and parts, so you can pick parts without reading the text: one entry a role, {role, section, sections, voice or kit, text, let, gen}, where gen is the call that is just that part (gen@2 techno seed=42 section=drop part=bass) and let is what `let drop_bass = \u003cgen\u003e` writes (a let a bar). Put a part's gen in a let or on a part line of your own program to layer it, mutate it in an arrange or cross it with another seed; there it is fitted to your program's key and tempo unless it says key= and bpm=, and check reports it in refs. With part (a role) the answer gains that part and its let alone; generator picks the version (1, 2 or 3, as gen@1, gen@2 and gen@3 name them; 3 writes BeatScript 2); seeds (a list, or {count, start}, at most 16) answers a compact summary a seed instead of one program.\n\nLexicon harvest, a side effect with no flag: when the program checks and may be kept (no samples or loops, no `use restricted`, public visibility; push_private records nothing), the (word, phonemes) pairs of its lyrics are recorded to grow the CC0 lexicon: each annotated escape word=/phonemes/ (jingle=/j ii ng g uh l/ sings the escape and keeps the word) and each word no lexicon knows, with the rules' pronunciation. Only the word, its phonemes and the program's CID are kept, never the text. The answer's lexicon is {harvested, new}: the pairs taken from this program, and how many no program had given before. When check says a word is not in the lexicon and it sounds wrong, write it as word=/phonemes/.",
   "label": "generate",
   "term": "https://beatscript.app/ontology/concept/tool-beatscript-generate"
  },
  {
   "definition": "A starting BeatScript program for a genre, built from the BeatScript ontology's data for it (https://beatscript.app/ontology/, CC0, forked from the bitwrap ontology): its tempo, key (root and scale), swing, drum kit and Euclidean kick, snare and hihat rhythms, its bass, arp and melody voices, its first chord progression (the chords, bass, arp and melody are Roman numerals, so a `key` line moves them all), and its family's standard song structure, each section with the roles the family's section profile makes active and its usual length. Returns {text, cid, canonical, seconds, genre{genre, label, family, tempo, tempo_range, scale, kit, voices, progressions, sections}} (and phrase, warnings with a phrase). The text passes check (it is checked before it is returned) and carries a `#! genre:` line, so check reports its style against the genre. Edit it, check it, then play or share it. For vocals, pass phrase (a phrasebook id from list_examples with phrasebook true, or any words): it is sung on the melody part of each section that has one, and the answer gains phrase with its source, year, meter, stress, syllables and phoneme escapes and whether its meter fits the genre (https://beatscript.app/docs/found-phrases.md). For a classical form, pass form (quodlibet, passacaglia, canon, rondo, fugue or theme-and-variations): the program is that form's template at the genre's tempo, built from public-domain themes, with the phrase (or the form's own) sung in it, and the answer gains form (its sections, mutation plan, themes and reference track) and expanded (the program written out to edit). To borrow one theme instead, pass theme (an id from list_examples with themes true): its lets go in, moved to the genre's key, played by the part of its role. See https://beatscript.app/docs/forms.md. For a whole track made from a phrase, write `arrange from template(deep-house, \"your words\")` (see expand). The plain answer also has use: each section of the genre's frozen template as a line, use genre@1 techno.drop as drop, that borrows it by name into a program of your own. Read-only, no sign-in. The answer also has expanded_cid (the CID of the program written out: the same as cid for a program with no references) and, for a program with references (a form's template(...)), refs and resolution, as check gives them: with roles bound, the template's entry in resolution has parts, each role (at any depth: a form in a role has its own) with the CID of what it is written out as. A form takes its sources as roles (docs/beatscript/ext-roles.md): with form and roles, each role is bound to a source (a seed of the seeded generator, a public-domain theme, notes, a stored program's part or section, another form), and the forms include cross (a quodlibet of two sources, roles a and b): two seeds that lead in turn, overlap, and morph toward each other and back.\n\nLexicon harvest, a side effect with no flag: when the program checks and may be kept (no samples or loops, no `use restricted`, public visibility; push_private records nothing), the (word, phonemes) pairs of its lyrics are recorded to grow the CC0 lexicon: each annotated escape word=/phonemes/ (jingle=/j ii ng g uh l/ sings the escape and keeps the word) and each word no lexicon knows, with the rules' pronunciation. Only the word, its phonemes and the program's CID are kept, never the text. The answer's lexicon is {harvested, new}: the pairs taken from this program, and how many no program had given before. When check says a word is not in the lexicon and it sounds wrong, write it as word=/phonemes/.",
   "label": "genre_template",
   "term": "https://beatscript.app/ontology/concept/tool-beatscript-genre-template"
  },
